module lib/nn
  The pieces a transformer is made of: RMSNorm, ReLU squared, softmax, one
  head of causal self-attention, the embedding lookup and cross-entropy, each
  a forward pass. The
  backward passes come from `value_and_grad` over them,
  and the tests check each generated gradient against central differences of
  its forward pass. The backward passes written by hand before
  `value_and_grad` existed are in lib/nn_adjoints.

  A row of a matrix is a position of the sequence and a column is a feature,
  so a sequence of `T` tokens in `D` numbers each is a `T` by `D` matrix.
  Each def reads `T` and `D` from the shapes of what it is given.

row_rms  @ba2b08fa
  what   The root mean square of each row, with `NN_EPS` inside the root: the
         divisor RMSNorm uses. The mean runs along the last axis, and the
         result keeps that axis, of length one, so it divides the rows it came
         from by broadcasting.
  why    The epsilon keeps a row of zeros from dividing by zero.
  X      the rows, `T` by `D` (or `B` by `T` by `D`)

  $$ r_i = \sqrt{\tfrac{1}{D} \textstyle\sum_j x_{ij}^2 + \epsilon} $$

rmsnorm  @e794080d
  what   RMSNorm with no learnable gain: divide each row by the root mean square of
         its own entries.
  why    It fixes the size of each position's vector without changing its
         direction, so what a layer reads does not depend on how large the stream
         has grown. Unlike LayerNorm it does not subtract the mean, and the
         speedrun keeps no gain, so there is nothing to learn.
  X      the rows to normalize, `T` by `D` (or `B` by `T` by `D`)

  $$ y_{ij} = \frac{x_{ij}}{r_i}, \qquad r_i = \sqrt{\tfrac{1}{D} \textstyle\sum_j x_{ij}^2 + \epsilon} $$

relu2  @7ca0b5e0
  what  `max(x, 0)^2`, elementwise: ReLU squared, the MLP's nonlinearity.
  why   Its derivative, `2 max(x, 0)`, is continuous at zero, and it costs one
        comparison and one multiplication; the speedrun uses it in place of GELU.
  X     the input
  R     `max(x, 0)`, entry by entry

softmax_rows  @31f05f6b
  what   Softmax along each row: exponentiate every entry and divide by the row's
         sum, so each row becomes positive numbers that add up to one. The row
         maximum is subtracted first so that nothing overflows. A row is the
         last axis, so a stack of matrices is done matrix by matrix.
  why    Subtracting the same number from a whole row does not change its softmax,
         and after subtracting the maximum the largest exponential is `exp(0)`.
  X      the scores, `T` by `D`, or a stack of such matrices
  e      the exponentials, each row shifted by its largest entry

  $$ y_{ij} = \frac{e^{x_{ij} - m_i}}{\sum_k e^{x_{ik} - m_i}}, \qquad m_i = \max_k x_{ik} $$

attention  @3db0c754
  what    One head of causal self-attention. Each position's row is mapped three
          ways, to a query, a key and a value. Position i scores every position j
          by the dot product of its query with j's key, the scores of later
          positions are hidden, a softmax turns each row of scores into weights,
          and position i's output is the weighted average of the values. It
          returns the output; `attention_weights` returns the weights.
  how     `Q = X Wq`, `K = X Wk`, `V = X Wv`; scores `Q K^T / sqrt(H)`, with
          `K^T` the last two axes of `K` swapped; everything
          above the diagonal set to `MASKED`; softmax along each row; output
          `A V`.
  why     This is the only place in a transformer where positions exchange
          information, and the weights are computed from the data, so which
          position a token draws from depends on what the tokens are. Dividing
          the scores by `sqrt(H)` keeps them from growing with the head's width,
          which would make the softmax put all the weight on one position before
          training starts. The mask is what makes the model causal: a position
          may not read the token it is being trained to predict.
  X       the rows attention reads, `T` by `D`, or `B` by `T` by `D` for a
          batch of sequences, each attended to on its own
  Wq      the query projection, `D` by `H`
  Wk      the key projection, `D` by `H`
  Wv      the value projection, `D` by `H`
  T       the number of positions
  Q       the queries, one row per position, `H` columns
  K       the keys, one row per position
  V       the values, one row per position
  S       the scores, `T` by `T`: `S[i, j]` is the dot product of position i's
          query with position j's key, scaled
  S@mask  the scores with every entry above the diagonal replaced by `MASKED`
  future  `1` where j > i, a later position, and `0` elsewhere: the mask as a matrix
  A       the attention weights: row i is a distribution over the positions i
          may read
  Y       the output, `T` by `H`: row i is the average of the values weighted by
          row i of `A`

  project  compute the queries, keys and values
    $$ Q = X W_q, \qquad K = X W_k, \qquad V = X W_v $$
  scores   score every pair of positions: query against key, scaled by the head's width
    $$ S = \frac{Q K^\top}{\sqrt{H}} $$
  mask     hide the future: a position may not attend to a later one
    why      a score of `MASKED` becomes a weight of exactly zero after the softmax
  weights  turn each row of scores into weights that sum to one
    $$ A_{ij} = \frac{e^{S_{ij}}}{\sum_k e^{S_{ik}}}, \qquad \sum_j A_{ij} = 1 $$  [hold "each row of attention weights sums to 1"]
    $$ A_{ij} = 0 \quad \text{for } j > i $$  [hold "no weight falls on a later position"]
  mix      each position's output: the values averaged with its weights
    $$ Y = A V $$

attention_weights  @4ac1f4e7
  what    The weights of one head of causal self-attention, without the output:
          the first four steps of `attention`, done the same way, so the
          weights are the ones `attention` mixes the values with. For a reader
          who wants to see where each position looked, and for the checks.
  X       the rows attention reads, `T` by `D`, or `B` by `T` by `D`
  Wq      the query projection, `D` by `H`
  Wk      the key projection, `D` by `H`
  Q       the queries, one row per position
  K       the keys, one row per position
  S       the scores, scaled
  future  `1` where j > i, a later position, and `0` elsewhere

cross_entropy  @0fa373fb
  what    Cross-entropy of `logits` against one target class per row, averaged
          over the rows: the mean of $-\log p$, where p is the probability the
          softmax of the row gives its target. The classes run along the last
          axis, so `logits` is `T` by `V` for a sequence, `B` by `T` by `V` for a
          batch, with one target per row in `target`.
  how     The softmax is folded in: the log-probabilities of a row are its logits
          minus `log(sum exp)`, computed with the row maximum shifted out so a
          large logit does not overflow. The target's entry of each row is
          picked out by its flat index, row times `V` plus the target.
  why     $-\log p$ is zero when the model is certain and right and grows without
          limit as p goes to zero. A model that spreads its probability evenly
          over V classes scores $\log V$. Its gradient with respect to the logits
          is the predicted distribution with one taken off the true class,
          divided by the number of rows, so each row of the gradient sums to
          zero.
  logits   the scores, one row per position
  target   the right class of each row, a whole number
  classes  `V`, the number of classes: the length of a row
  top      each row's largest logit
  logp     the log-probabilities, row by row

embedding  @7b9051bd
  what  The rows of a table the ids name, one row per id: the lookup of a
        token or position embedding. A matrix of ids (a batch of sequences)
        gives one row per id in the same arrangement.
  why   A token is a row number. Picking the row is what multiplying a one-hot
        row by the table computes, without the multiplication; the gradient
        adds into the rows that were picked and leaves the others at zero.
  W     the table, one row per id
  ids   the ids, whole numbers

argmax_rows  @21f93f79
  what    The class each row of `logits` (`T` by `V`) picks: the index of its largest
          entry, the first one on a tie. This is the model's guess, which is what an
          accuracy counts.
  logits  the scores, `T` by `V`
  best    each row's pick so far
  i       a row
  j       a class

worst_rel  @df55265e
  what  The largest gap between two matrices of one shape, relative to the larger
        of the two entries, which is how a gradient check should be scored.
  A     one matrix
  B     the other

central  @c15ef617
  what  The gradient of `f(X, C)` with respect to `X` by central differences: move
        each entry up and down by `h` and divide the change in `f` by `2h`. The
        tests hold every generated gradient to it.
  f     the function, of a matrix and a fixed second argument
  X     where to take the gradient; each entry is moved and put back
  C     the second argument, held fixed
  h     the step
  num   the gradient, one entry per entry of `X`
  i     a row
  j     a column
  was   the entry before it was moved
  up    `f` with the entry moved up
  dn    `f` with the entry moved down

nn_test_matrix  @1de4799b
  what  A fixed matrix with no structure, `rows` by `cols`: the corner of lib/linalg's
        square test matrix.
  rows  its rows
  cols  its columns
  seed  which test matrix
  M     the square test matrix it is cut from

rmsnorm_probe  @be07ee4a
  what  A probe loss for checking RMSNorm's gradient: the sum of `C * Y` entry by
        entry, whose gradient with respect to `Y` is `C`. Any fixed `C` with no
        structure will do.
  X     the rows normalized
  C     the fixed weights

relu2_probe  @870d8f11
  what  The same probe loss for ReLU squared.
  X     the input
  C     the fixed weights

softmax_probe  @a9a4513e
  what  The same probe loss for the row softmax.
  X     the scores
  C     the fixed weights

embedding_probe  @7c436b1b
  what  A weighted sum of an embedding from `W` that looks one row up twice
        and another never, so the gradient test sees a row that adds two
        contributions and a row that gets none.
  W     the table
  C     the fixed weights

attention_case  @2a8d6431
  what  The inputs the attention checks share: five positions of width five, and
        three projections to width three.

attention_probe  @0d4ecc4a
  what  The probe loss of attention's output, as a function of the whole case:
        its gradient is an `AttentionCase` holding the gradient with respect to
        the rows read and to each of the three projections.
  a     the rows read and the three projections
  C     the fixed weights

central_into  @172a48fd
  what  The central difference of the probe loss in every entry of one matrix
        of the case, written into the same entries of another: what
        `central_case` calls on each matrix.
  w     a matrix of the case; each entry is moved and put back
  nw    the same matrix of the result
  C     the fixed weights
  a     the case, which reads `w` where it is
  h     the step
  i     a row
  j     a column
  was   the entry before it was moved
  up    the probe loss with the entry moved up
  dn    the probe loss with it moved down

central_case  @e9677e9e
  what  The gradient of the probe loss with respect to every matrix of the
        case, by central differences: an `AttentionCase` of numerical
        gradients.
  a     the case
  C     the fixed weights
  num   the numerical gradients

central_slab  @512c94e0
  what  The gradient of `batch_attention_probe` with respect to the matrix of
        sequence `b` in a batch, by central differences.
  X     the batch of sequences; each entry of sequence `b` is moved and put back
  C     the fixed weights
  b     which sequence
  h     the step
  rows  the sequence's positions
  cols  its features
  num   the gradient, one entry per entry of the sequence
  i     a position
  j     a feature
  was   the entry before it was moved
  up    the probe with the entry moved up
  dn    the probe with it moved down

test_batch  @600b7ce1
  what  A batch of `NN_TEST_BATCH` fixed matrices with no structure, `rows`
        by `cols` each, from consecutive seeds: what the batch tests run on.
  rows  each sequence's positions
  cols  its features
  seed  the seed of the first sequence's matrix
  S     the batch
  b     a sequence

batch_attention_probe  @6314809b
  what  The probe loss of attention's output on a batch of sequences, with the
        projections of `attention_case`: its gradient is the gradient with
        respect to every sequence of the batch.
  X     the batch of sequences
  C     the fixed weights
  a     the case whose projections are used
